(3362,3362,1,'A Multimodal Artificial Intelligence Framework for Information Integration and Intelligent Decision Support','<p>As the amount of information grows, there is an emerging need for AI systems that can integrate multiple modalities of information into a coherent, reliable, and interpretable decision-making tool. A multimodal artificial intelligence framework is created that learns textual, acoustic, and visual information in a modality-independent way, utilizes cross-modal attention, and adapts fusion and confidence-aware classification. The synthetic dataset of 8000 data points was divided into training, validation, and testing sets in the ratio 70:15:15. The evaluation measures used to assess the framework were: accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, area under the receiver operating characteristic curve, baseline comparison, modality-wise assessment, ablation testing, and error analysis. The proposed framework resulted in an accuracy of 95.1%, precision of 95.3%, recall of 95.2%, F1 score of 95.2%, and AUC of 98.9%. It outperformed the Logistic Regression, Random Forest, MLP and Multimodal Transformer models. Text was the best unimodal source with 88.0% accuracy, while adaptive fusion was 94.0%, which showed that the acoustic and visual information could complement each other. Removing the adaptive gating and modality dropout lowered the accuracy by 6.3 and 6.2 percentage points, respectively. The confusion matrix revealed that there were 550 true negative observations, 591 true positive observations, 29 false positive observations, and 30 false negative observations. The results suggest that the use of adaptive modality weighting and cross-modal interaction can enhance predictive discrimination, robustness, and error balance. The framework provides a scalable basis for intelligent decision support with heterogeneous and incomplete information. The assessment was done with synthetic data; however, testing with real data sets, with different populations and decision contexts, must be done before deployment.</p>','["Multimodal artificial intelligence", "information integration", "adaptive fusion", "cross-modal attention", "decision support", "classification"]',NULL,29,1,'247 - 259',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-10',2),
(3696,3696,1,'Web-Based Artificial Intelligence Model for Predicting Complications After CT-Guided Transthoracic Lung Tumor Biopsy','<p>Computed tomography-guided transthoracic lung biopsy is an important minimally invasive technique for obtaining tissue from peripheral pulmonary lesions. However, pneumothorax and pulmonary parenchymal hemorrhage remain frequent complications, and accurately estimating an individual patient’s risk before and during the procedure remains challenging. This study aimed to develop a web-based artificial intelligence-enabled prediction model for estimating the risks of pneumothorax and pulmonary parenchymal hemorrhage after computed tomography-guided transthoracic lung biopsy. A retrospective dataset of 141 patients who underwent the procedure at Binh Dan Hospital between June 2019 and June 2023 was analyzed. Multivariable logistic regression models incorporating lesion size, lesion depth from the pleural surface, and number of needle passes were developed and implemented in a web-based clinical decision-support tool to provide individualized complication-risk estimates. The mean patient age was 63.87 years, 70.9% were male, and 65.2% had a history of smoking. Pneumothorax occurred in 29.7% of patients, with chest drainage required in 2.1%, while pulmonary parenchymal hemorrhage occurred in 46.1% and hemoptysis in 7.8%. The prediction models achieved accuracies of 79.4% for pneumothorax and 85.1% for pulmonary parenchymal hemorrhage. Smaller lesions, greater lesion depth, and a higher number of needle passes were associated with increased complication risk. The developed web-based model converts readily available anatomical and procedural variables into immediate individualized risk estimates. It may support patient counseling, procedural planning, optimization of needle sampling, and postbiopsy monitoring; however, external and prospective validation is required before routine clinical implementation.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Clinical decision support", "Computed tomography-guided transthoracic lung biopsy", "Pneumothorax", "Prediction model", "Pulmonary parenchymal hemorrhage"]',NULL,29,1,'2575 - 2584',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-17',3),
(3020,3020,1,'Sustainable Human Resource Management Practices and Organizational Competitive Advantage','<p>Sustainable Human Resource Management (SHRM) has become a new strategic way of managing to support organizations to be competitive in the long term while considering the economic, social, and environment responsibilities. This research focuses on the sustainable human resource management practices and their effect on the competitive advantage of the organization, which was conducted quantitatively. The information was obtained from primary data, which were gathered from 200 respondents in the form of structured questionnaire from the organizations that have adopted sustainable HR practices. Data collected were analyzed and presented descriptively, Cronbach''s alpha, exploratory factor analysis (EFA), Pearson correlation, and multiple regression analysis (MR) were performed using IBM SPSS Statistics. The results show that SHRM practices are positively and significantly related to OCA. The reliability and validity analyses showed that the measurement scales were reliable and valid for empirical studies. The correlation analysis showed that there is a positive relationship between the practices of SHRM and organizational competitive advantage, and the regression analysis indicated that the variables of the sustainable HR practices were positively and significantly influenced the enhancement of the organization''s competitive advantage in increasing employee commitment, innovation, knowledge sharing, and enhancing organizational resilience. The study is in the direction of the Resource Based View (RBV) which emphasizes on the employees as valuable strategic resources that generate sustainable competitive advantage. The findings offer managers and policymakers practical recommendations that highlight the need for the formulation of human resource policies and practices that incorporate sustainability. Longitudinal research designs and more participants are encouraged in future studies to further validate and extend the results.</p>','["Sustainable Human Resource Management (SHRM)", "Organizational Competitive Advantage", "Sustainable HR Practices", "Resource-Based View (RBV)", "Employee Commitment", "Organizational Innovation", "Knowledge Sharing", "Organizational Resilience", "Competitive Performance", "SPSS Analysis."]',NULL,28,1,'01 - 14',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-07',1),
(3021,3021,1,'AI-Driven Digital Ecosystems: Integrating Marketing Intelligence, Supply Chain Analytics, FinTech Innovation, Legal Governance, and Social Dynamics','<p>Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly revolutionizing the digital world of businesses by combining the factors of consumer behavior, marketing information, finances, social connections, and operations to make intelligent decisions. This research explores how AI-based analytics can create a vision of integrated decision intelligence in current digital ecosystems and how it can be used in customer churn prediction, marketing, FinTech, supply-chain decision support, social dynamics and responsible governance. A quantitative machine learning method was used on a dataset of 1000 observations of customers and 25 demographic, behavioural, marketing, financial and social variables. To evaluate these five predictive algorithms, accuracy, precision, recall, F1 score and ROC-AUC were used. Significant distinctions in digital engagement, cart abandonment, customer-service interactions, purchase inactivity, and social-media engagement emerged between the churned and non-churned customers. The overall predictive performance of XGBoost was the highest with 91.5% accuracy and the highest ROC-AUC of 0.978. The feature-importance analysis showed that customer-service calls, lifetime value and cart abandonment rate are among the top factors that predict churn. The results show that AI can help to translate all the different information that customers can provide into something useful for marketing, financial and operational decisions. The study also highlights the importance of integrated, AI-powered ecosystems featuring transparent, privacy-aware, fair, and accountable governance frameworks that guarantee responsible and sustainable intelligent decision-making across interconnected digital business functions.</p>','["Artificial Intelligence", "Digital Ecosystems", "Marketing Intelligence", "FinTech Innovation", "Intelligent Decision-Making"]',NULL,28,1,'15 - 28',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-07',2),
(3022,3022,1,'Cloud–Edge Computing Architecture for Intelligent Real-Time Data Processing in Smart Enterprise Applications','<p>Smart enterprise applications generate large volumes of network and operational data that require rapid, accurate, and resource-efficient processing. This study proposes an intelligent cloud–edge computing architecture for real-time enterprise network traffic analysis and intrusion detection. The architecture combines lightweight edge-based processing with advanced cloud analysis through a confidence-based task-offloading mechanism. High-confidence predictions are processed locally at the edge, whereas uncertain records are forwarded to the cloud for further classification. The proposed approach was evaluated using the TON_IoT network dataset, which initially contained 211,043 records and 44 attributes. After removing 20,569 duplicate records, 190,474 unique records were used for model development and testing. Logistic Regression, Decision Tree, and Random Forest classifiers were assessed for binary attack detection, while Decision Tree was also evaluated for multiclass classification. Random Forest achieved the best binary classification performance, with 99.84% accuracy, 99.86% precision, 99.94% recall, and a 99.90% F1-score. The Decision Tree achieved 98.47% multiclass accuracy and a macro F1-score of 96.07%. At a confidence threshold of 0.80, 99.67% of records were processed at the edge, while only 0.33% were offloaded to the cloud. The results demonstrate that the proposed architecture can maintain high detection performance while reducing cloud workload and communication requirements.</p>','["Cloud\\u2013edge computing", "Smart enterprise applications", "Real-time data processing", "Machine learning", "Intrusion detection."]',NULL,28,1,'29 - 54',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-07',3),
(2696,2696,1,'Beyond the Overall Strength Index: Behavioral and Emotional Strength Profiles Among Kazakh Adolescents in Bayan-Ölgii Province, Mongolia','<p>Strength-based assessment can reveal competencies that are not captured by deficit-focused approaches, yet a global composite score may conceal lower strengths in specific subscales. This study examined behavioral and emotional strengths among 314 Kazakh adolescents (160 boys, 154 girls; ages 11–19 years) in Bayan-Ölgii Province, Mongolia, using a Kazakh-language administration of the Behavioral and Emotional Rating Scale–Second Edition (BERS-2). The mean Strength Index was 99.58 (SD = 15.56), and 72.9% of participants were classified as Average or higher. However, among adolescents with an Average-or-higher Strength Index, 33.6% had at least one Below Average or Poor subscale score. Internal consistency was good across the five subscales (α = .806–.859), although a correlated five-factor confirmatory model showed poor absolute fit, χ²(1264) = 5365.99, CFI = .536, TLI = .513, RMSEA = .105, and SRMR = .092. Parallel analysis suggested a more complex empirical structure. A MANCOVA showed a significant multivariate gender effect across the five subscales, Wilks’ Λ = .788, F(5, 307) = 16.51, p &lt; .001, whereas age was not significant. Within the Average-or-higher group, a higher Strength Index strongly reduced the odds of hidden subscale-specific vulnerability, OR = 0.14 per 10-point increase, 95% CI [0.08, 0.24]. These findings support subscale-level interpretation of strengths and indicate that the Kazakh-language BERS-2 requires further psychometric evaluation before validation can be claimed.</p>','["adolescents, behavioral and emotional strengths, BERS-2, Kazakh, Mongolia, strengths-based assessment"]',NULL,27,1,'01 - 15',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-02',1),
(2697,2697,1,'Structural-Adaptive and Resilience-Driven Hybrid Deep Learning Framework for Intrusion Detection in Autonomous Vehicular Networks','<p>Safety-critical choices depend on real-time V2V, V2I, and vehicle communications, therefore autonomous vehicular networks need intrusion detection systems with structural awareness, temporal sensitivity, and adversary drift resistance. Connected ECUs and edge intelligence have extended the cyber-attack surface, but most IDS frameworks are signature-driven, centrally taught, or data-centric. Distribution shift, adversarial perturbations, poor contextual reasoning, and limited scalability plague federated vehicular situations. In dynamic attack intensification sets, present systems prioritize classification accuracy over structural vulnerability propagation and cyber-resilience elasticity. A five-stage analytical approach for validated autonomous car network intrusion detection is described here. First, AVTC-CEGM constructs a structural assault surface topology vector from Temporal Causal Entropy Divergence to characterize communication instability. Spatio-Temporal Adversarial Drift Modeling using Physics-Aware Diffusion Transformers (STADM-PADT) calculates the Vehicular Adversarial Drift Coefficient with vehicular dynamics limitations. Third, HQ-GMNIR attributes contextual anomalies using coherence collapse analysis. Fourth, trust-entropy aggregation and reinforcement-based response control reduce latency and packet loss in FTT-POM. Finally, the HRV-CRBE formalizes multi-dataset stress resilience elasticity. A thorough analysis of vehicle and network intrusion datasets indicates improved detection accuracy (97%+), fewer false positives, faster federated convergence, and resilience to high Intensity attacks. The architecture turns IDS research from anomaly detection to structurally informed, adversarially robust, and system-level cyber-resilient intelligence for next-generation autonomous mobility.</p>','["Autonomous Vehicular Networks, Causal Entropy Graph Mining, Adversarial Drift Modeling, Federated Intrusion Detection, Cyber-Resilience Engineering, Analysis"]',NULL,27,1,'16 - 37','10.17762/jidmis.v3.2697',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-02',2),
(2698,2698,1,'Generative AI in the VFX Pipeline: Productivity Gains, Skill Shifts, and Ethical Challenges — An Indian Perspective','<p>Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI) tools—including text-to-image diffusion models, AI-driven inpainting, automated rotoscoping, neural rendering, and compositing assistants—are rapidly integrating into global visual effects (VFX) pipelines. While international studios report workflow experimentation and partial automation gains, empirical evidence on productivity, role transformation, and ethical governance remains limited, particularly within India’s service-dominant VFX sector. This explanatory sequential mixed-methods study investigates the measurable productivity impacts, skill reallocation, and ethical challenges associated with generative AI adoption in Indian VFX studios. Phase I consists of a national survey (~200 respondents) measuring adoption rates, estimated time savings, task shifts, training needs, and governance practices. Phase II includes 15–20 semi-structured interviews and three in-depth case studies capturing pipeline changes, validation practices, and intellectual property (IP) considerations. Quantitative analysis employs descriptive statistics, paired t-tests, regression modeling, and factor analysis, while qualitative thematic coding provides cross-case synthesis. The study aims to (1) quantify productivity gains across tasks; (2) map evolving professional roles; (3) catalogue ethical and IP risks; and (4) propose a studio-level AI governance framework and reskilling roadmap. Findings will inform industry stakeholders, educators, and policymakers seeking responsible AI integration in VFX production.</p>','["Generative AI, Visual Effects, Productivity Analysis, Skill Transformation, Creative Industries, AI Ethics, Mixed-Methods Research"]',NULL,27,1,'38 - 44',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-09-02',3),
(2284,2284,1,'An Enhanced Triangle Algorithm for Large-Scale Support Vector Machine Optimization: A Comparative Study with Classical and Modern Solvers','<p>Support vector machines (SVMs) are established maximum-margin classifiers, but the quadratic-programming structure of SVM training can become computationally demanding when dimensionality and sample size increase. This article revisits the Triangle Algorithm, a geometry-based method that determines convex-hull intersection or separation and approximates the optimal supporting hyperplanes for the hard-margin SVM problem. The reorganised article presents an improved implementation perspective, which requires the same four implementation mechanisms from the source MATLAB study: closest-point updates of two line segments, caching of dot products, an anti-zig-zag pivot strategy, and prioritised searches over non-bounding points, but also introduces the published Triangle Algorithm. The original numerical tests are kept unchanged in order to ensure the tracking of the new journal to validated original results. The convex-hull intersection, dimensional scaling, and inter-hull distance sensitivity were examined using two randomly generated unit balls. The average number of iterations required for 10,000 dimensions and 26.53 s for 5,000 points in each set was 4.95 when doing intersection testing. Use of the Triangle Algorithm at 10,000 dimensions with optimal support took 36.39 s, whereas for Sequential Minimal Optimisation (SMO) this time was 165.99 s, with the reported support distances being almost equal in both cases. For increasing distance between convex hulls, Triangle Algorithm runtime decreased from 5.14 s to 2.745 s, while SMO remained in the range 67.014 s – 118.436 s. These results demonstrate the feasibility of geometry-based SVM optimisation, and inspire improved implementation methods. The approach is also placed in the current debate in comparison to LIBSVM, LIBLINEAR, ThunderSVM, stochastic subgradient, multilevel and modern large-scale kernel solvers.</p>','["Support vector machine", "Triangle Algorithm", "sequential minimal optimization", "convex hull", "large-scale optimization", "geometric optimization", "hard-margin classification"]',NULL,26,1,'01 - 15',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-27',1),
(2323,2323,1,'Strategic Performance Measurement Using the Balanced Scorecard in Digital Media Organizations: A Study of Organizational Effectiveness','<p>This paper investigates the use of Balanced Scorecard (BSC) as a strategic performance measurement instrument in the improvement of the organizational effectiveness in digital media houses. A quantitative research design was used and sampled 135 respondents by giving them a structured questionnaire using a structured approach. The study underlines the idea that the importance of non-financial performance indicators grows due to the digitally-friendly environment, which is marked by rapid innovation and customer-focus. Such results suggest that the Balanced Scorecard provides an overall framework of incorporating not only the strategic objectives but also operational performance and consequently improve the operations of decision-making in digital media organisations and sustainable competitive advantage.</p>','["Balanced Scorecard, Strategic Performance Measurement, Organizational Effectiveness, Digital Media Organizations, Customer Perspective, Learning and Growth, Digital Transformation"]',NULL,26,1,'16 - 23',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-27',2),
(2324,2324,1,'Evidence-Based Classroom Decision-Making through Educational Analytics: A Study among Secondary School Teachers','<p>Currently, educational analytics is becoming more of an approach in supporting for evidence-based classroom decision making because of its ability to apply educational data for enhancing teaching and learning. The study adopted the quantitative descriptive and analytical approach in order to examine the aspects of educational analytics for the secondary school teachers.A quantitative descriptive and analytical research design was involved in this study. A structured questionnaire was used and 300 secondary school teachers were sampled in this research by stratified random sampling technique to obtain primary data. A conclusion from the findings is that early learning teachers have a positive attitude toward educational analytics and see its potential in lesson planning, assessment of students and in monitoring the classroom. Hypothesis testing suggested that teacher training, institutional support, and experience in teaching have significant impact on effective use of educational analytics. The research findings indicate that improving digital infrastructure assets and professional development will have a positive impact on digital classroom effectiveness and digital instructional practices.</p>','["Educational Analytics, Evidence-Based Decision-Making, Classroom Decision-Making, Secondary School Teachers, Data-Driven Teaching, Learning Analytics."]',NULL,26,1,'24 - 35',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-27',3),
(1966,1966,1,'Sustainable Operations Management through Data-Driven Inventory Classification and Material Requirement Planning','<p>The increasing complexity of supply chains and the growing emphasis on sustainability have necessitated the development of more adaptive and data-driven inventory management approaches. The traditional inventory management systems fail to address the demand uncertainty, cost-effectiveness and resource utilization at the same time. The current paper will propose a hybrid concept of sustainable operations management that will be grounded in the integration of the ABC-XYZ classification of the inventories and optimization principle of Material Requirement Planning (MRP). The differentiation of inventory items occurred by applying economic value and demand variability with the set of 3,204 stock-keeping units, and thus, it was possible to apply various control strategies. These results reveal that the goods of high value and that has steady demand are concentrated in the major part and must be monitored first and planned properly. Demand variability is an inclusion in classification and it enhances the decision making by taking into consideration the uncertainty in the replenishment process. Besides, the optimization of safety stock and reorder point improves the inventory balance by reducing excess stock and stock out stock. Inventory turnover affects the business performance positively as observed in the analysis whereas holding cost affects the business performance negatively and the inventory should be used efficiently. The study is relevant to the existing literature as it brings to one platform classification, forecasting sensitivity, and optimization of planning. The findings provide practical insights for developing resilient and sustainable inventory systems in dynamic supply chain environments.</p>','["Inventory Optimization", "ABC\\u2013XYZ Classification", "Material Requirement Planning", "Demand Uncertainty", "Sustainable Supply Chain"]',NULL,25,1,'01-10','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1966',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',1),
(1967,1967,1,'Beyond Accounting on Capital Adequacy: Capital Quality, Capital Quantity, and Risk Density in Bank Resilience Evidence from the Jordanian Banking System','<p>This study examines how bank capital should be interpreted when a regulatory capital ratio combines economically distinct dimensions of balance-sheet strength. Using source-level financial-soundness files issued by the Central Bank of Jordan, the analysis constructs a bank-year panel for 2015-2019 and separates three components of capital strength: capital quality, measured as core or Tier 1 capital relative to total regulatory capital; capital quantity, measured primarily as total regulatory capital relative to total assets; and risk density, measured as risk-weighted assets relative to total assets. The validated descriptive sample contains 121 bank-year observations, and the one-year-ahead fixed-effects estimations contain 96 observations. Capital quality is exceptionally high and compressed, averaging 97.9% of total regulatory capital. In conventional bank and year fixed-effects models, capital quantity has larger point estimates and greater incremental explanatory content than capital quality, although small-cluster wild-bootstrap inference does not support a general claim of quantity dominance. The clearest result emerges from decomposing the conventional risk-weighted capital ratio. After controlling explicitly for risk-weighted-asset density, a one-within-bank-standard-deviation increase in capital quantity is associated with a 0.78 percentage-point reduction in next-year non-performing loans, with a wild-cluster-bootstrap p-value of 0.039. By contrast, the headline total-capital-to-risk-weighted-assets ratio changes sign and provides little robust predictive information, indicating that denominator variation can materially alter the economic interpretation of capital adequacy. Formal nonlinear tests reject a purely linear treatment of capital quality and identify turning regions around a core-capital share of 97%, although the estimated shape does not imply a simple monotonic saturation mechanism. Interaction tests provide no evidence that capital quality attenuates the NPL-profitability relationship, while the positive association between capital quantity and future profitability weakens as risk density rises. The evidence supports a three-part supervisory interpretation of capital strength: capital composition, capital intensity relative to total assets, and RWA density should be assessed jointly rather than inferred from a single headline ratio.</p>','["bank capital", "capital quality", "capital quantity", "Tier 1 capital", "risk-weighted assets", "bank resilience", "non-performing loans", "Basel III", "Jordan"]',NULL,25,1,'11-38','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1967',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',2),
(1968,1968,1,'Assessing Trust in Conversational Agents through Sentiment, Emotion, Response Quality, Latency, and Engagement - A Mixed Synthetic and Human-Intervened Dataset Study','<p>In this agentic era, trust in conversational agents plays a critical role in their adoption and effectiveness across diverse applications. This research focuses on assessing trust in conversational agents by analyzing the impact of sentiment, emotion, response quality, latency, and user engagement. Synthetic and human-intervened datasets were generated using a range of proprietary and open-source large language models (LLMs), including GPT-4, Claude, Llama, Gemini, and others. A total of 3,909 conversations with 26,329 turns were created, simulating diverse conversational scenarios. Trust evaluations were conducted by trained human annotators (n=15) using a standardized framework, scoring perceived competence, benevolence, integrity, and overall trustworthiness on a 1–7 scale. Inter-annotator agreement was established with Cohen’s Kappa coefficients (target κ &gt; 0.75). Sentiment analysis and emotion detection were employed to derive trust metrics, while contextual factors influencing trust judgments were documented qualitatively. The findings reveal that sentiment and emotion significantly influence trust perceptions, while response latency impacts user satisfaction. Engagement patterns, such as conversation length and emotional variation, were found to correlate with trust levels. The research also developed a modular codebase to support reproducible experimentation and extensibility, enabling integration of new models and metrics. This study provides insights into designing trustworthy conversational agents and contributes to the broader field of trust engineering by offering methodologies for quantifying and optimizing trust in AI systems.</p>','["Comparative Sentiment Analysis", "Public Opinion", "Stunting", "K-Nearest Neighbor", "Na\\u00efve Bayes", "Decision Tree."]',NULL,25,1,'39-58','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1968',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',3),
(1596,1596,1,'Performance Comparison of K-Means and Expectation-Maximization Clustering Algorithms for Network Intrusion Detection','<p>The tremendous growth of networks and internet-based services have led to more numerous and complicated cyberattacks. Organizations today widely adopt networks for many things; like sending messages, transferring money, data storing, and using internet for communication and cloud computing. Therefore, networks are the attractive targets for the cyber-attacks and require protective mechanisms. Firewalls, antivirus program, and access controls provide the preventative means, but they are only enough for new attacks or simple kinds of attacks, they cannot provide any protection to sophisticated or newly discovered kinds of attacks. Intrusion detection systems are an unavoidable system for a cybersecurity environment, since they continuously monitor network traffic and detect malicious activity that the prevention mechanisms miss out. Machine learning methods have gained a considerable success at the field of network intrusion detection since they can analyse and learn patterns from network traffic, unlike rule-based systems, they do not require predefined signature of attacks. There are two types of machine learning algorithms, supervised learning and unsupervised learning. Unsupervised learning is more efficient for network intrusion detection and particularly Clustering as a type of unsupervised learning is used since it can help in partitioning the network traffic into various types of connection with identifying the anomalous connection without using the pre-labelled intrusion training data. The most popular methods in clustering are the K-means algorithm for its high calculation efficiency and ease of implementation, while expectation-maximization (EM) algorithms provide probabilistic model based-clustering which can model more complex kind of data. Only very few research has compared the two types of clustering, K-Means and EM algorithms for network intrusion detection system under exactly the same experimental settings and the same common dataset, The present study outlines the comparative analysis of K-Means and Expectation-Maximization clustering algorithms towards network intrusion detection through applying on the KDD Cup 1999 benchmark dataset. Experiments is setup using WEKA data mining tool for having a consistent research environment. Comparison research based on the computationally performance, clustering patterns, converging behaviours, and the execution efficiency in general manner. Data for network traffic is pre-processed and then the two clustering algorithms are analysed using exactly same parameters where appropriate. Comparison metrics for two algorithms includes the runtime, distribution patterns across clusters, speed of convergence, and clustering validity. The primary research intention is to assess the strengths and weakness of the K-means and EM algorithms for an unsupervised detection of intrusions, thus providing a guide for an appropriate clustering technique for large networks information security system.</p>','["Intrusion Detection System, Network Security, Machine Learning, Unsupervised Learning, K-Means Clustering, Expectation-Maximization, WEKA, KDD Cup 1999, Cybersecurity, Data Mining."]',NULL,24,1,'01-14','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1596',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',1),
(1615,1615,1,'A Novel Iterative Machine Learning-Driven Framework for Reliable and Adaptive Cloud Data Migration','<p>Cloud data migration has become an essential requirement for modern enterprises due to the rapid growth of cloud computing and data-intensive applications. However, achieving reliable and efficient migration remains challenging because of heterogeneous data types, fluctuating network bandwidth, diverse migration tools, and the need to preserve data consistency throughout the migration process. Most existing approaches evaluate migration tools independently, assume static network conditions, and provide limited support for adaptive optimization and verifiable migration outcomes. This paper presents AMF-CloudForge, a unified machine learning-driven framework that integrates migration state analysis, intelligent scheduling, consistency preservation, and real-time adaptive management into a single end-to-end architecture. The proposed framework begins with Polytype Migration State Encoding (PMSE), which performs comprehensive micro-benchmarking across different data types, file sizes, and network conditions to generate Migration State Tensors and Tool Efficiency Scores that accurately characterize migration behavior. These learned representations are then utilized by the Topology-Aware Diffusion Scheduler (TADS) to optimize chunking, routing, and migration tool selection by considering dynamic network topology, bandwidth variations, and transfer costs. To ensure data integrity and consistency, the Causal Delta with Erasure-coded Commitment (CDEC) module combines causal dependency tracking, adaptive erasure coding, and cryptographic quorum verification to guarantee reliable and verifiable data commits without data loss or metadata inconsistencies. Furthermore, the Uncertainty-Quantified Twin Supervisor (UQTS) continuously monitors the migration process through a probabilistic digital twin, enabling risk-aware scheduling adjustments and real-time Service Level Objective (SLO) verification under changing system conditions.Together, these components form a fully automated migration framework capable of policy-driven execution and machine-verifiable migration reporting. Experimental evaluation on heterogeneous cloud workloads demonstrates that the proposed framework reduces overall migration makespan by 35–45%, sustains effective bandwidth utilization above 85%, and limits unrecoverable data loss to approximately 10⁻¹¹ per 10 TB of migrated data. By integrating machine learning-based performance modeling with adaptive scheduling, cryptographic consistency verification, and intelligent runtime supervision, the proposed framework transforms cloud data migration from a static, tool-centric process into a reliable, adaptive, and continuously optimized cloud service.</p>','["Cloud Data Migration, Machine Learning Scheduling, Causal Consistency, Erasure Coding, Automated Framework, Process."]',NULL,24,1,'15-38','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1615',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',2),
(1616,1616,1,'AI-Powered Fraud Detection in Banking: A Big Data Analytics Perspective','<p>Banking fraud presents a persistent challenge in the digital era, with financial losses continuing to escalate as transaction volumes grow exponentially. This paper examines AI-enabled fraud detection frameworks that leverage big data analytics, machine learning, and deep learning methodologies. The review systematically categorizes existing approaches across supervised learning, anomaly detection, ensemble methods, and deep neural network architectures. Special attention is directed toward hybrid frameworks that combine multiple techniques to address extreme class imbalance, concept drift, and real-time processing constraints. Analysis indicates that ensemble models incorporating XGBoost with LSTM networks achieve accuracy exceeding 98% with substantially reduced false positive rates. Critical challenges including model interpretability, data privacy, and deployment scalability are examined, alongside promising directions for future research in this domain.</p>','["Big data analytics", "banking fraud detection", "machine learning", "deep learning", "ensemble methods", "anomaly detection", "hybrid models."]',NULL,24,1,'39-51','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1616',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-14',3),
(1370,1370,1,'The Effect of Lean Manufacturing on Customer Satisfaction through Service Quality','<p>Purpose – The aim of current research is to identify service quality as a mediator variable between lean manufacturing and customer satisfaction of the state company of Textile &amp; Leather Industries in Iraq.</p>
