<p>Design/methodology/approach – The sample group for the research consist of individuals from the state company for Textile &amp; Leather Industries in Iraq. The sample involved 160 individuals and was designed depending on a stratified and proportional distribution. The scale of Lean manufacturing presented in this study was found to be applicable and well-matched. The linking between the quality service dimensions found on the real perception of the state company for Textile &amp; Leather Industries in Iraq, through the individual, with the individuals’ satisfaction to their organizations, was investigated using SEM.</p>
<p>Findings – The plethora of originality value statements recognized in the works are analytically presented. Moreover, significant topics of the originality value of the LM studies are formulated.</p>
<p>Research limitations/implications.</p>
<p>Practical implications – the originality value of the existing LM, QS, and CS studies and presenting respective meaningful themes can help researchers and practitioners design their future research and implementation plans, respectively.</p>
<p>Originality value – The relationships among the variables studied are a subject that has not been reviewed in the literature previously.</p>','["Lean manufacturing, Service Quality, customer loyalty, Textile & Leather Industries."]',NULL,23,1,'01 - 11','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1370',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',1),
(1371,1371,1,'The Influence of Green Supply Chain Management Practices on Sustainability Performance in the Sultanate of Oman Industries: The Mediating Role of Environmental Collaboration','<p>Green supply chain management (GSCM) has become a strategic requirement for industrial firms seeking to reduce environmental harm while improving long-term sustainability performance. Despite growing interest in green procurement, eco-design, green manufacturing, and green distribution, limited empirical evidence explains how these practices are converted into sustainability performance through collaborative environmental action in the Sultanate of Oman. Guided by the Natural Resource-Based View, this study examines the effects of four GSCM practices on environmental collaboration and investigates the mediating role of environmental collaboration in the relationship between GSCM practices and sustainability performance. A quantitative cross-sectional survey was conducted among procurement, manufacturing, logistics, and supply chain professionals working in Omani industries. From 520 questionnaires distributed, 342 valid responses were retained for analysis. The data were analyzed using Smart PLS 4.0 and partial least squares structural equation modelling. The measurement model demonstrated acceptable reliability, convergent validity, and discriminant validity. The structural results show that eco-design, green procurement, green manufacturing, and green distribution have significant positive effects on environmental collaboration. Environmental collaboration, in turn, has a strong positive effect on sustainability performance. The indirect effects also support the mediation of the relationships between the GSCM practices and the sustainability performance, confirming the mediation chain. The results indicated that green practices are not enough; they have to be integrated with the collaboration of suppliers, customers, logistics and other functions within the company to make green practices produce results in terms of performance. The study adds to GSCM and sustainability literature by providing a relational mechanism that determines the contribution of green practices to the industrial sustainability of the Oman context.</p>','["Green Supply Chain Management, Environmental Collaboration, Sustainability Performance, Eco-design, Green procurement, Green manufacturing, Green distribution, Oman."]',NULL,23,1,'12 - 31','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1371',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',2),
(1372,1372,1,'Validation of a Policy Framework for Support Programs of Iraqi Sports Organizations to Promote the Marketing of Olympic Sports: A Case Study of Swimming','<p>Introduction: The sports industry, particularly Olympic sports, requires systematic support programs for the development of marketing activities. This study aimed to design a policy model for supportive programs of sports organizations in Iraq regarding marketing activities in Olympic sports, with a case study of swimming.</p>
<p>Methodology: This study employed a sequential exploratory mixed-method approach. In the qualitative phase, the systematic grounded theory method was utilized, and data were collected through semi-structured interviews with 22 experts. In the quantitative phase, the extracted model was tested using a researcher-made questionnaire and structural equation modeling with Smart-PLS software on a sample of 384 participants.</p>
<p>Findings: The final model indicated that supportive policymaking (the core phenomenon) comprises two main categories: "supportive programs" and "marketing activities." This phenomenon is influenced by causal conditions (managerial, economic, socio-cultural, and educational factors). The extracted strategies are shaped by contextual and intervening conditions, leading to outcomes such as swimming development, strengthening international relations, and socio-economic transformations. The quantitative results also confirmed the model''s fit (GOF=0.433).</p>
<p>Conclusion: The proposed model can serve as a guiding framework for Iraqi sports policymakers towards systematic development of marketing and attracting effective support in Olympic sports.</p>','["Supportive Programs, Sports Marketing, Swimming, Policymaking, Grounded Theory, Iraq."]',NULL,23,1,'32 - 39','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1372',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-08',3),
(1320,1320,1,'Hybrid Decision-Support Framework for Prioritising PMMVY Awareness Outreach among Marginalised Women in Rural Bharatpur','<p>Programmes for maternal welfare can be widely enrolling and yet failing to reach those who are still aware and able to access but who have not yet done so. This study is about the lack of an operational mechanism for the identification and prioritisation of such women in the context of Pradhan Mantri Matru Vandana Yojana (PMMVY). A hybrid explainable decision-support framework was proposed, combining the Gower-distance k-medoids clustering, entropy weighting, TOPSIS ranking and a shallow decision-tree surrogate. Primary survey data was collected from 300 PMMVY beneficiaries in a rural area of Bharatpur, Rajasthan, based on their demographic characteristics, awareness, access, digital literacy and institutional dependence. The analysis resulted in six stable clusters of beneficiaries, Gower silhouette = 0.585 and bootstrap agreement = 0.959. The 62 women were ranked at high priority, 101 as moderate priority and 137 as routine priority by TOPSIS. Access friction and awareness deficit were the most significant factors in prioritisation, with several high-need groups being distinguished by educational vulnerability. The decision tree successfully replicated the priority categories at high fidelity and macro-F1&gt;0.91, while the sensitivity analysis demonstrated good ranking stability. The study finds that the success of the aggregate programme may mask the need for targeted outreach and that it provides a clear and documented mechanism for equitably distributing resources for PMMVY outreach.</p>','["PMMVY, beneficiary segmentation, entropy-TOPSIS, explainable decision support, maternal welfare."]',NULL,22,1,'01 - 18','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1320',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',1),
(1321,1321,1,'The Role of Bonding Social Capital and Nature-Based Water Management in Enhancing Ecotourism Resilience: A Case Study of Loksado in the Meratus Mountains','<p>This study investigates how bonding social capital and nature-based water management jointly enhance the resilience of Loksado ecotourism in the Meratus Mountains, South Kalimantan. The Amandit River Basin faces hydrological degradation, and the Dayak Loksado community’s indigenous social capital remains poorly integrated into formal water governance. We hypothesize that bonding capital directly and indirectly strengthens ecotourism resilience through its influence on nature-based solutions (NbS) for water management, with bridging capital acting as a partial mediator. A quantitative survey was conducted among 168 respondents across four villages in Loksado District. Data were analyzed using Structural Equation Modeling-Partial Least Squares (SEM-PLS). Our results show that bonding capital positively and significantly predicts NbS-based water management (β=0.421, p&lt;0.001), which in turn significantly predicts ecotourism resilience (β=0.387, p&lt;0.001). Bridging capital partially mediates the relationship between bonding capital and resilience. The model explains 64.3% of the variance in ecotourism resilience (R^2=0.643), with all structural paths exhibiting large effect sizes (f^2&gt;0.35). The total effect of bonding capital on resilience is 0.359, while the total effect of NbS-based water management is 0.554. Hence, NbS-based water management serves as a partial mediator. This research contributes empirical evidence that indigenous social cohesion and traditional ecological knowledge are foundational, not merely ancillary, to sustaining ecotourism in hydrologically sensitive tropical mountain regions. The findings underscore the necessity of integrating community-level social capital with nature-based water strategies to bolster ecotourism resilience against environmental threats.</p>','["ecotourism", "indigenous knowledge", "nature", "social capital", "water management"]',NULL,22,1,'19 - 39','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1321',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',2),
(1322,1322,1,'Religious Moderation, Digital Literacy in Social Cohesion Interaction among the University Students in Indonesia','<p>This paper investigates the interplay between digital literacy, religious moderation, and social cohesion among university students in Indonesia, a context marked by increasing digital religious consumption and concerns about polarization. We propose and test a mediation model wherein digital literacy—particularly algorithmic awareness and critical source evaluation—predicts religious moderation, which in turn predicts social cohesion. The study employs a multi-method design integrating a cross-sectional survey of 1,200 students across STEM, social sciences, and Islamic studies, semi-structured interviews with 48 participants, content analysis of 200 student-curated religious items on WhatsApp and Instagram, and simulated platform analysis of TikTok and YouTube recommendation systems over 14 days. Our results demonstrate that the relationship between digital literacy and religious moderation varies significantly across academic disciplines due to distinct epistemic cultures; for instance, STEM students emphasize procedural verification, while Islamic studies students combine textual authority with interpretive tradition. Furthermore, peer-to-peer digital sharing networks function as informal curation systems that can either strengthen or weaken institutional moderation efforts. The evidence supports a mutually reinforcing pathway rather than a unidirectional causal chain: digital literacy enables moderation indirectly through source criticism and resistance to algorithmic reinforcement, while moderation is associated with stronger social cohesion when supported by dialogue and institutional framing. The strongest protective factor across disciplines is the combination of epistemic culture, digital literacy, and institutional support for moderation. This research contributes a multi-level framework that integrates individual competencies with structural factors, offering actionable insights for designing targeted interventions in Indonesian higher education that strengthen both digital competencies and social harmony.</p>','["digital literacy, religious moderation, social cohesion, university students."]',NULL,22,1,'40 - 66','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1322',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-08-21',3),
(1068,1068,1,'Reproducible Performance Evaluation of a Hybrid Hadoop–Spark Big Data Architecture Using Urban Mobility and Global Event Datasets','<p>This study presents a reproducible framework for evaluating the performance of Big Data workflows inspired by Hadoop–Spark processing logic using two open datasets with distinct workload characteristics. The first dataset, the NYC TLC Yellow Taxi Trip Record Data for January 2023, represents an urban mobility scenario and contains temporal and spatial trip attributes. The second dataset, the GDELT Event Daily Export for 1 January 2023, represents global event data with temporal, geographic, categorical, and quantitative characteristics. Three reproducible processing configurations are compared: a disk-based batch baseline, a Spark-only workflow, and a Prepared-storage + Spark workflow, which serves as a storage-oriented approximation of a hybrid Hadoop–Spark processing pipeline. The experimental design includes batch aggregation, spatial–temporal analysis, stream-like processing, scalability testing, and task restart-based fault recovery. Performance is evaluated using execution time, throughput, latency, scalability behavior, CPU utilization, RAM delta, I/O-related behavior, and recovery time metrics. To ensure reproducibility, a unified preprocessing procedure was applied across all configurations. The TLC dataset was reduced from 3,066,766 to 2,883,546 valid records, while the GDELT dataset was reduced from 43,575 to 42,053 valid records. The study does not claim universal superiority of the storage-oriented workflow. Instead, its primary contribution lies in providing a transparent and reproducible methodology for comparing storage-oriented and Spark-based data processing workflows using heterogeneous open datasets under identical preprocessing rules and comparable workload execution conditions.</p>','["Big Data", "Hadoop", "Apache Spark", "HDFS", "hybrid architecture", "performance evaluation", "urban mobility", "GDELT", "reproducible benchmarking", "scalability."]',NULL,21,1,'01 - 41','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1068',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',1),
(1069,1069,1,'Behavioral Segmentation in Digital Payment Platforms Using Temporal-Adaptive Attentive Fuzzy Clustering','<p>Digital payment platforms record a wealth of transaction data, capturing intricate and dynamic patterns of user behavior. Clustering users according to their behavioral patterns presents unique challenges due to overlapping behavior sets, dynamicity, and unlabeled data. In this paper, we propose a temporal-adaptive attentive fuzzy clustering method for behavioral user segmentation in digital payment platforms. We construct a behavioral representation for each user based on transaction records using a temporal decay function to emphasize recent behaviors. An attention mechanism is incorporated into the fuzzy clustering objective to adaptively assess the importance of each behavior feature on the clustering results. We employ a soft assignment approach that allows each user to have varying degrees of membership across multiple clusters to capture the gradual transition of payment behaviors. We evaluate our approach on a publicly available digital wallet transaction dataset. Our results show that the proposed clustering method achieves better clustering quality and temporal consistency compared to traditional clustering algorithms. The experimental results show that incorporating temporal adaptivity and attention provides a promising and effective basis for behavioral clustering in digital payment platforms.</p>','["Behavioral segmentation", "digital payments", "fuzzy clustering", "temporal decay", "attention mechanism", "unsupervised learning."]',NULL,21,1,'42 - 52','10.59543/jidmis.v3.1069',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-07-31',2);
