(183,183,1,'Assessing the Accuracy and Reliability of Artificial Intelligence in First Permanent Molar Extraction Decisions','<p>The first permanent molar (M1) plays a critical role in maintaining functional occlusion during mixed dentition. When this tooth is severely compromised by deep caries, structural weakness, or developmental enamel defects, extraction may be required. However, the decision to extract M1 is influenced by multiple prognostic factors, including the child’s age, dental development stage, second molar (M2) angulation, and the presence of third molars. In this context, artificial intelligence (AI)–based conversational systems can support clinical judgment and patient education by offering quicker access to relevant information. This descriptive-analytical study evaluated the performance of two AI-powered language models (ChatGPT-3.5 and ChatGPT-4) in providing clinical guidance on M1 extraction. Ten standardized questions were formulated based on established prognostic parameters associated with spontaneous space closure. Both AI systems produced coherent and clinically relevant content. However, ChatGPT-4 tended to produce longer responses with greater sentence complexity, resulting in lower readability and higher academic demand. By comparison, ChatGPT-3.5 produced shorter, more straightforward responses that may be more appropriate for communication with parents or patients. Future research incorporating varied question structures, such as case-based scenarios, radiographic-based questions, and real-user feedback, may help define how conversational AI can be safely and effectively integrated into pediatric dental workflows. Until then, AI-based tools should be used as supportive resources rather than as independent clinical decision-makers, and their outputs should be reviewed by qualified professionals to ensure patient safety.</p>','["Artificial intelligence", "Pediatric dentistry", "First permanent molar", "Extraction decisions", "Decision support"]',NULL,15,1,'540-553','10.59543/bcx2xp86%20',NULL,NULL,NULL,'published','2026-02-28',2),
(209,209,1,'AI Pair Programming and Knowledge Sharing in Developer Communities','<p>With the increasing availability of artificial intelligence-supported pair programming tools in professional software development, past research has largely concentrated on their impact on individual developers. Much less is known about their potential impact on developer communities. Specifically, we still lack empirical insight into whether the proliferation of artificial intelligence coding assistants is associated with observable changes in community participation, collaborative communication, and externalized knowledge creation across large-scale developer platforms. In this paper, we introduce the Loneliness Framework, a socio-technical process model comprising six mechanisms that link AI pair programming to community-level behavioral change, and examine whether statistically significant longitudinal shifts in platform-trace measures of developer community behavior are temporally correlated with the adoption of artificial intelligence-supported pair programming. We employ an observational, non-causal longitudinal design and analyze two real-world data sets: a Stack Overflow data set from the Stack Exchange Data Dump and a public GitHub events data set from GH Archive, spanning January 2018 to December 2024. We analyze nine monthly behavioral metrics through trend, period-based comparison, correlation, and effect-size analyses, using false discovery rate correction for the primary hypothesis tests. We find that all nine metrics exhibit statistically significant longitudinal changes. The largest changes include decreases in question volume, pull request discussion density, and documentation-related behaviors, together with increases in median time to first answer. Many of these changes became most pronounced after late 2022, when large language model-based coding assistants became widely available. We also find positive relationships among selected documentation-related metrics, indicating coordinated shifts in externalized knowledge behaviors. Overall, the results indicate statistically detectable changes in developer community behavior during the era of artificial intelligence-supported programming. However, because this is an observational study, the findings should be interpreted in a descriptive, non-causal manner.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/gk2qp136">https://doi.org/10.59543/gk2qp136</a></p>','["AI pair programming, developer communities, Loneliness Framework, knowledge externalization, collective intelligence, software engineering, longitudinal analysis"]',NULL,15,1,'554-571','10.59543/gk2qp136',NULL,NULL,NULL,'published','2026-03-19',3),
(6260,6260,1,'Healthcare Quality and Awareness of Diabetes Mellitus Among the Population of the Al-Baha Region, Saudi Arabia: A Cross-Sectional Analytical Study','<p><strong>Background:</strong> Community health literacy is a cornerstone of chronic disease control under Saudi Vision 2030. This study evaluated diabetes awareness and its relationship with perceived healthcare quality among residents of the Al-Baha Region, Saudi Arabia.</p>
<p><strong>Methods:</strong> An observational, community-based, cross-sectional analytical study was conducted among 2,000 residents across the Sarawat and Tihamah sectors of Al-Baha. Data were collected using a validated, self-administered questionnaire measuring diabetes awareness across five domains and perceived healthcare quality across five dimensions. Descriptive statistics were calculated, and inferential analyses included independent-samples *t*-tests, one-way analysis of variance, and Pearson correlation coefficients using SPSS version 26.0. Statistical significance was set at *p* &lt; 0.05.</p>
<p><strong>Results:</strong> The aggregate correct response rate for diabetes awareness was high (94.95%). However, a critical knowledge gap was identified regarding asymptomatic onset, with 19.5% of participants expressing uncertainty. Perceived healthcare quality was rated highest for the availability of educational programs (mean = 3.98/5) and lowest for chronic disease follow-up pathways (mean = 2.89/5). Diabetes awareness was significantly and positively correlated with perceived healthcare quality (*r* = 0.412, *p* &lt; 0.001). Residents of the mountainous Sarawat sector reported significantly lower perceived healthcare quality than residents of Tihamah (*p* = 0.008), although diabetes awareness did not differ significantly between sectors (*p* = 0.341). Educational attainment was significantly associated with diabetes awareness (*p* &lt; 0.001).</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion:</strong> Cognitive public awareness of diabetes is strong in the Al-Baha Region, but systemic execution—particularly continuous clinical monitoring and chronic disease follow-up—requires strategic enhancement. Regional interventions should address geographic disparities in perceived healthcare quality and optimize automated patient-recall and structured follow-up infrastructures.</p>','["Diabetes awareness", "healthcare quality", "Al-Baha", "cross-sectional study", "Saudi Arabia", "chronic disease management"]',NULL,38,1,'01 - 09','10.17762/jidmis.v2.6260',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-11-13',1),
(6354,6354,1,'Benefits of Interprofessional Coordination in Early Sepsis Care: A Narrative Review of Recognition, Diagnostic Processes and Antibiotic Delivery','<p><strong>Background and Aims.</strong> Delayed antibiotics in sepsis are associated with higher mortality. Delivering the first dose depends on hand-offs between nursing, physicians, laboratory and pharmacy, yet they appear to be examined together less often. This review aimed to examine published evidence on where delays occur between departments in the first hours of adult sepsis care, and which coordination strategies have been evaluated and with what benefit.</p>
<p><strong>Methods.</strong> Narrative review of original studies, quality-improvement reports and international guidelines, organised by step in the care pathway. PubMed/MEDLINE and supplementary sources were searched between 22 October and 5 November 2025 for English-language studies published from 2006 to 2024.</p>
<p><strong>Results.</strong> In a large multicentre cohort, the median recognition interval (triage to antibiotic order) was substantially longer than the median administration interval (order to infusion), although the former cannot be attributed to a single hand-off. Order-to-administration delays were common (half of patients in one 4,429-patient emergency department cohort waited more than 1.3 hours) and had an association with mortality similar in magnitude to that of ordering delays. Nurse-initiated protocols, point-of-care lactate and emergency department pharmacist involvement improved process measures, mostly in single-centre studies. An early warning alert shared by providers and the emergency department pharmacist shortened time to antibiotics in a randomised quality-improvement study, but no study compared coordinated with single-department approaches.</p>
<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The available evidence supports treating early sepsis care as a cross-departmental process and measuring time to antibiotics by interval. Multicentre evaluations of coordinated interventions are needed, with time-stamps fine enough to attribute delay to specific hand-offs, including in Saudi and Gulf hospitals.</p>','["sepsis", "time-to-treatment", "interprofessional relations", "nursing", "pharmacy service, hospital", "clinical laboratory services"]',NULL,38,1,'10 - 18',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-04-15',2),
(6431,6431,1,'Health Service Coordination During Mass-Casualty Preparedness: A Scoping Review of Staff Roles, Training and Simulation','<p>Background: Mass-casualty incidents (MCIs) test the whole health system at once: emergency medical services, hospital departments, primary care, public health surveillance and psychosocial services must act together under pressure. Exercises and simulations are the main way health systems rehearse that coordination, but most published work focuses on hospital emergency departments and clinicians. Less is known about how technical, administrative and psychosocial staff, and services outside the hospital, are prepared.<br/>Objective: To map how exercises and simulations have prepared clinical, technical and administrative teams to coordinate across hospitals, primary care and public health services during MCIs, and to identify gaps relevant to Saudi Arabia''s health-cluster model.<br/>Methods: A scoping review following the Arksey and O''Malley framework, as refined by Levac et al. and the Joanna Briggs Institute, and reported according to PRISMA-ScR. Peer-reviewed and grey literature published between January 2000 and December 2025 was searched. Sources were included if they described, evaluated or reported participation in MCI exercises, drills or simulations, or role-specific preparedness training, involving at least one clinical, technical or administrative staff group and at least two service levels. Data were charted on exercise format, staff roles, service interfaces, coordination mechanisms and reported outcomes, then synthesised descriptively and thematically.<br/>Results: Thirty-two sources were included: 10 reviews, 8 exercise or simulation studies, 6 cross-sectional surveys, 5 case reports or commentaries, and 3 grey-literature reports or theses. Eight came from Saudi Arabia. Hospital-based full-scale, functional and hybrid exercises dominated, with emergency department staff, physicians, nurses and paramedics as the main participants. Coordination was usually measured through command and control, communication, triage and surge flow. Evaluations rarely went beyond participant reaction and process observation. Operating room, anaesthesia and pharmacy staff appeared mainly as surge-capacity constraints rather than as trained coordination partners. Primary care was largely absent from exercises, even though reverse triage and community surge depend on it. Psychologists, social workers and mental health facilities were rarely included in exercise design, so responder psychological first aid and family assistance were often not planned. Public health points of entry were exercised separately from hospital MCI drills.<br/>Conclusions: The evidence describes hospital-centred rehearsal more than whole-system coordination. Health clusters in Saudi Arabia are well placed to run cluster-level exercises that put hospitals, primary care centres, mental health services, EMS and points-of-entry surveillance in the same scenario. They should give explicit exercise roles to technical, administrative and psychosocial staff, and evaluate exercises at the level of behaviour and system change.</p>','["mass-casualty incidents", "disaster preparedness", "simulation", "exercises and drills", "interprofessional coordination", "primary health care", "public health", "psychosocial support", "Saudi Arabia", "health clusters"]',NULL,38,1,'19 - 37',NULL,NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2025-04-15',3),
(162,162,1,'Construction of New Similarity Measures for Complex Pythagorean Fuzzy Sets and Their Applications in Decision-Making Problems','<p>Complex Pythagorean fuzzy sets (CPFSs) extend Pythagorean fuzzy sets by expressing membership, non-membership, and hesitancy degrees using complex numbers. An urgent issue in CPFSs is determining how to accurately measure the similarity between sets. Similarity measures are crucial for assessing the closeness between two objects and are extensively applied in decision-making. In this paper, we propose new similarity measures based on trigonometric functions and their weighted representations. Additionally, we investigate the properties these measures satisfy and demonstrate their effectiveness through several numerical examples. Lastly, we apply these similarity measures to decision-making problems, including pattern recognition and medical diagnosis.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11737">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11737</a></p>','["Complex Pythagorean fuzzy sets, similarity measures, trigonometric function, decision-making"]',NULL,14,1,'156–173','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11737',NULL,NULL,NULL,'published','2025-01-03',1),
(163,163,1,'Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) using Neutrosophic Best Worst Method (BWM) : A Case Study of Helicopter Assembly Line for Military Aviation Area','<p>One of the most popular structured approaches in risk assessment is Failure Mode and Effects Analysis (FMEA) that helps in discovering potential failures existing within the design of a product or process. Determining the risk weights of failure types correctly is a critical part of the decision-making process of the analysis. In this study, the Best Worst Method (BWM) was integrated with the Neutrosophic Fuzzy Set and applied to the FMEA problem. The aim of the study is to determine the failure types encountered in the helicopter assembly line in the military aviation field and to offer solution suggestions. A case study was conducted for AH-64E Apache type helicopters for the effectiveness of the proposed method. The results show that gap and mismatch for structural elements, molds should not be used appropriately, unnecessary materials should be brought to the assembly line and attention should be paid to cabling problems.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> h<a>ttps://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.12605</a></p>','["FMEA, BWM, MCDM, Neutrosophic Fuzzy Set, Helicopter"]',NULL,14,1,'174–185','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.12605',NULL,NULL,NULL,'published','2025-01-10',2),
(164,164,1,'A multi-criteria decision-making framework for addressing youth unemployment in developing countries: Pathways to sustainable solutions','<p>Youth unemployment is among Africa’s most urgent challenges, with over 60% of its population under 25, the largest youth demographic globally. This youthful population presents a significant opportunity to drive economic growth, innovation, and development. However, the lack of sufficient employment opportunities risks perpetuating poverty, inequality, and social instability. To address this issue and unlock Africa’s potential, this study evaluates and prioritizes six strategies identified through a comprehensive literature review and expert input. Using the Stepwise Weight Assessment Ratio Analysis (SWARA) method within an interval-valued spherical fuzzy (IVSF) framework, the research facilitates collective decision-making. It highlights three key strategies as the most appropriates: reforming education systems to align skills with labor market demands, fostering growth in sectors such as agriculture, manufacturing, and tourism, and promoting entrepreneurship to empower Africa’s young innovators. The study offers actionable insights for policymakers to effectively combat youth unemployment and maximize the continent’s demographic advantage.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11647">https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11647</a></p>','["Youth unemployment, Strategy, IVSF, SWARA, Decision-making analysis, Africa"]',NULL,14,1,'186–196','10.59543/jidmis.v2i.11647',NULL,NULL,NULL,'published','2025-01-24',3),
(6403,6403,1,'The Importance of Preventing Doctors, Nurses, and Assistants from Being Exposed to Infection While Working Within a Healthcare Facility','<p>Background: Healthcare professionals such as doctors, nurses and healthcare assistants are at risk of coming into contact with infectious agents through contact with patients, invasive procedures, contaminated equipment, clinical waste and respiratory secretions. Prevention of exposure to infection is vital for the protection of workers in order to ensure continuity of care. Objective: To identify the occurrence and patterns of occupational exposures and infections and how they can be prevented amongst healthcare professionals in Saudi Arabia. Methods: A narrative synthesis was performed using a set structure. Ten published empirical studies from Saudi Arabia were identified using publicly available sources from PubMed and publishers. Data were collected on occupational injuries, respiratory infections and infection prevention strategies. Recommendations for infection prevention were based on a Saudi Ministry of Health environmental health guideline. Results: A nationwide survey found that 22.2% of participating healthcare workers had suffered at least one sharps/needle injury within the last year. A study in Jazan found that 30% of healthcare workers in secondary hospitals and 14% in tertiary hospitals reported injuries. Nurses accounted for 56.0% and housekeepers for a reported 8.9% of recorded injuries at a hospital in Makkah (n=524). A national seroprevalence study found 2.9% seropositivity in referral hospitals compared to 0.8% in control hospitals (n=12,621). A multifaceted hand hygiene strategy was implemented in Abha and compliance increased from 60.8% to 86.4%. Conclusion: Prevention of occupational infections should be multifaceted with strategies in place such as the use of safer devices, hand hygiene, correct PPE, vaccination, prompt reporting and fair protection of ancillary staff. Evidence from Saudi Arabia suggests that infection prevention should be implemented across the whole facility.</p>','["occupational infection", "healthcare workers", "infection prevention and control", "needlestick injuries", "personal protective equipment", "patient safety", "Saudi Arabia."]',NULL,39,1,'01 - 10','10.17762/jidmis.v1.6403',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2026-11-18',1),
(154,154,1,'Culture and Conflict Resolution in Cross-Cultural Projects: Insights from Questionnaires and Interviews','<p>As conflict management gained more importance in cross-cultural project management, this paper explores conflicts and conflict resolution strategies in real-world cross-cultural projects, emphasizing the significant influence of cultural differences. Conducting a mixed-method approach that combines questionnaires and interviews, this study identifies various types of conflicts: communication conflicts, cooperation conflicts, value conflicts, status conflicts, and relationship conflicts. Then, this paper classifies conflict resolution strategies according to five conflict management styles: competing, cooperating, compromising, avoiding, and accommodating. The findings reveal that cultural differences can have dual effects on cross-cultural project dynamics. While they can lead to conflicts and challenges, they also present opportunities for growth and innovation. The results, supported by data from prior studies in cross-cultural projects, demonstrate that understanding and managing these conflicts is crucial for project success. Furthermore, the study''s findings are generally valid and potentially transferable across different contexts within project management despite some limitations, such as a smaller sample size and a focus on internal rather than external conflicts. This research contributes to academic knowledge and practical applications, offering insights for improving conflict resolution strategies and enhancing cross-cultural collaboration in project management. Future research could address the identified limitations by expanding the scope of the study and focusing more on external conflicts and specific situational dynamics.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v1i.11127</p>',NULL,NULL,13,1,'01-24','10.59543/jidmis.v1i.11127',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2024-10-26',1),
(155,155,1,'Predicting Duration of Residential Units’ Sale Using Machine Learning Techniques','<p>Real estate is vital, meeting basic needs and offering solid investments. Informed investment decisions require understanding home sale factors. Estimating residential property sales time can boost rewards and decrease risks. Two steps are involved in this paradigm. Significant characteristics are identified using filter weighting and regression techniques. K-nearest Neighbour, Naïve Bayes, and Decision Trees use characteristics identified in the first stage. Determining the most efficient model requires comparing their precision. The study provides brokers with insights for improved sales forecasting and transaction management. This technique allows stakeholders to adjust their plans based on market fluctuations, potentially leading to more profitable real estate investments. This research clarifies real estate investment strategies for greater returns.</p>
<p><strong> DOI:</strong> https://doi.org/10.59543/jidmis.v1i.11409</p>',NULL,NULL,13,1,'25–44','10.59543/jidmis.v1i.11409',NULL,NULL,'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/','published','2024-12-05',2);
